To optimize the function for high-load scenarios and low CPU usage, you can consider the following approaches:

1. Use a caching mechanism to reduce frequent interactions with the NMS and improve response times. This can be achieved by storing the configuration parameters in memory and updating them only when necessary, reducing the number of requests sent to the NMS.
2. Implement a mechanism to detect and recover from partial updates, ensuring the configuration remains valid and functional even if an update fails mid-process. This can be done by using a transactional approach, where all updates are treated as a single unit of work and rolled back if any error occurs during the update process.
3. Use memory pools to manage dynamic updates efficiently and minimize fragmentation. Memory pools can help reduce the overhead of frequent memory allocations and deallocations, improving performance under high-load scenarios.
4. Design the system to support periodic synchronization with the NMS, balancing the need for up-to-date configurations against the overhead of frequent updates. This can be achieved by using a timer mechanism to periodically check for updates from the NMS and update the configuration structure accordingly.
5. Optimize the function to handle large configuration files efficiently, reducing the risk of out-of-memory errors and ensuring the system performs well under stress. This can be done by using a streaming approach, where the configuration file is read in chunks and processed as it is received, rather than loading the entire file into memory at once.
6. Implement logging and monitoring capabilities to track system health and performance, allowing for proactive maintenance and issue resolution. This can be achieved by using a logging framework that provides detailed information about system events, such as errors, warnings, and performance metrics.
7. Develop comprehensive unit tests and integration tests to ensure the function is working correctly under different scenarios and conditions. This can help identify and fix bugs early in the development process, improving the overall quality of the codebase.']

