This code implements the `generateAndConfigureNetworkFilesReport` function that processes a directory containing multiple files related to network configuration, categorizing them into text, binary, or empty types based on their content. The function initializes a network interface configuration structure with parameters like protocol version, packet size, buffer depth, and link quality indicator, ensuring they adhere to predefined constraints to optimize throughput and reliability. Robust error handling manages invalid inputs and unexpected conditions, allowing the system to recover or fallback gracefully. Log detailed validation steps and final configuration states for debugging and performance tuning.

The function constructs a new filename based on the original filename's characteristics, ensuring it includes a timestamp, a number of digits equal to half the length of the original filename (rounded down), and a file type determined by the last character. Use `sprintf` and `strcat` to build the new filename, ensuring it fits within 256 characters, even when including spaces and special characters. Allocate memory dynamically for the new filename, freeing it after successful construction. Consider system dependencies, integrating smoothly with a logging module that appends metadata, ensuring the new filename can accommodate this without exceeding the maximum length. Handle external data sources, addressing discrepancies in timestamps and ensuring consistent filename generation. Minimize time taken for filename generation under high-frequency calls from a web server, balancing performance and resource usage. Address integration issues with existing directory structures, ensuring the generated filename complies with specific naming conventions. Handle edge cases where the original filename ends with a valid file type or reaches the maximum allowed length, appending the timestamp and digits without altering the existing extension. If memory allocation fails, handle the error gracefully and return an appropriate status code.

Additionally, implement support for processing files in parallel to improve performance. Ensure thread safety and synchronization when accessing shared resources. Validate the integrity of the network configuration structure after each update, ensuring that all fields are correctly initialized and that the structure adheres to its constraints. Incorporate robust error handling and logging mechanisms to ensure graceful recovery in case of unexpected conditions or invalid inputs.']

