The method then iterates over the batch of strings and computes the MD5 hash for each one using the `digest` method of the `MessageDigest` instance. The resulting bytes are converted to a hexadecimal string using the `bytesToHexString` method, which is defined as a separate static method.

The `hashStrings` method returns a map where the keys are the original strings and the values are their respective MD5 hashes.

To handle high-load scenarios, this implementation uses a caching mechanism to store previously computed hashes and minimize redundant computations. The `hashes` map is initialized with an initial capacity of the maximum batch size, which allows it to grow as needed without resizing. This reduces the overhead of frequent resizing operations.

The method also ensures thread safety by using a synchronized block around the caching mechanism and the hash computation. This prevents concurrent modifications to the `hashes` map and ensures that only one thread can access the cache at a time, minimizing contention.

To support concurrent processing of multiple requests, this implementation uses a separate instance of the `StringHasher` class for each request. Each instance has its own copy of the `hashes` map, which allows it to process multiple requests simultaneously without interfering with other instances.

The method is also designed to be easily extensible, allowing for the addition of new hashing algorithms in the future. This can be achieved by adding a new case statement to the `switch` block in the `hashStrings` method, which would handle the computation of the new hash algorithm for each string in the list.

