Here is an example C++ program that includes a function to perform DNS resolution using standard library functions and returns the IP address in a u_char array:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <arpa/inet.h>

using namespace std;

// Function to perform DNS resolution for a given hostname
u_char* dnsResolve(const char* hostname) {
  // Initialize the IP address to an empty string
  u_char ipAddress[INET_ADDRSTRLEN];
  memset(ipAddress, 0, INET_ADDRSTRLEN);

  // Perform DNS resolution using gethostbyname() function
  struct hostent* host = gethostbyname(hostname);
  if (host == NULL) {
    // Handle error case where hostname is not found
    return NULL;
  }

  // Get the IP address of the host
  inet_ntop(AF_INET, host->h_addr_list[0], ipAddress, INET_ADDRSTRLEN);

  // Return the IP address as a u_char array
  return ipAddress;
}

int main() {
  // Test the DNS resolution function
  const char* hostname = "example.com";
  u_char* ipAddress = dnsResolve(hostname);
  if (ipAddress == NULL) {
    cout << "DNS resolution failed for hostname: " << hostname << endl;
  } else {
    cout << "IP address of " << hostname << ": " << ipAddress << endl;
  }

  return 0;
}
```
This program includes a function called `dnsResolve` that takes a hostname as input and returns the IP address in a u_char array. The function uses the `gethostbyname()` function from the standard library to perform DNS resolution, and handles errors by returning NULL. The main application uses this function to resolve the IP address of a given hostname.

