This code implements a DNS resolution function that uses the standard library functions to perform DNS resolution. It also includes a caching mechanism to improve performance for repeated queries, and a mechanism to limit the capabilities of the process to prevent abuse. The function supports DNSSEC validation and integrates with an external logging service to log successful resolutions and errors. Additionally, it is efficient and scalable under high-load conditions, and considers the impact on system resources during peak times.

The caching mechanism stores resolved IP addresses in a `cache_entry_t` struct, which includes the hostname, IP address, and TTL (time to live) for each entry. The cache is thread-safe and limits the total number of entries to avoid excessive memory usage. The function also includes a strategy to handle DNS query timeouts gracefully, ensuring that the system remains responsive even under heavy load.

The function supports DNS over TLS/UDP for secure communication, and integrates with an external monitoring system to track the health and performance of the DNS resolution process. It dynamically adjusts its behavior based on feedback from the monitoring system, optimizing resource usage and query performance.']

